lesson:
You have seen constructors: they run when an object is created. C++ also has the opposite, a **destructor**, which runs automatically when an object is destroyed. It has the class name with a `~` in front and takes nothing:

```
class SampleBuffer
{
public:
    SampleBuffer(int n) : data(new double[n]), size(n) {}   // acquire
    ~SampleBuffer() { delete[] data; }                      // release
private:
    double* data;
    int size;
};
```

When does it run? For a stack object, **at the closing brace of its scope**, no matter how you leave: falling off the end, a `return` in the middle, a `break`, or an exception. You can't forget to call it, because you never call it yourself.

```
void logWater()
{
    SampleBuffer buf(100);     // memory taken here
    if (pumpBroken()) return;  // ~SampleBuffer runs here
    ...
}                              // or here
```

This idea has a famous clumsy name: **RAII**, "Resource Acquisition Is Initialisation". Say it as: *the constructor acquires, the destructor releases, and the object's lifetime decides when*. A resource is anything you must give back: heap memory, an open file, a locked door, a network connection, a motor you switched on.

Why it matters: with raw `new`/`delete` every path out of a function needs its own cleanup, and one missed path is a leak. With RAII the cleanup is written **once**, in the destructor, and the compiler runs it on every path.

You already use RAII: `std::string` and `std::vector` free their memory in their destructors, and `std::ifstream` closes its file. The smart pointers in the next units are RAII for single heap objects.

**Members are destroyed too.** When an object dies, its destructor body runs first, then each member's destructor. So a class whose members are a `std::vector` and a `std::string` needs no destructor at all: they clean themselves.

**The common mistake:** a class that owns a raw pointer and can be **copied**. The default copy copies the pointer, both objects later delete the same memory, and the program crashes. Until unit 7 explains the fix, block copying:

```
SampleBuffer(const SampleBuffer&) = delete;
SampleBuffer& operator=(const SampleBuffer&) = delete;
```

In Unreal, `FScopeLock` locks a mutex in its constructor and unlocks it in its destructor; the same pattern guards robot motors in real tools: the "stop" goes in the destructor so an error can't leave a motor running.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Motor
{
public:
    Motor(std::string name) : name(name) { std::cout << name << " ON\n"; }
    ~Motor() { std::cout << name << " OFF\n"; }   // always runs
    Motor(const Motor&) = delete;
    Motor& operator=(const Motor&) = delete;
private:
    std::string name;
};

bool runPump(bool sensorFault)
{
    Motor pump("pump");
    if (sensorFault) {
        std::cout << "fault! leaving early\n";
        return false;            // ~Motor still runs: pump OFF
    }
    std::cout << "pumping water\n";
    return true;
}                                // ~Motor runs here on the normal path

int main()
{
    runPump(false);
    runPump(true);
    {
        Motor fan("fan");
        std::cout << "inside the block\n";
    }                            // fan OFF at this brace
    std::cout << "done\n";
    return 0;
}

